From ad72025d750c8696d3b357b4fd52a967801c5155 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wisp <wisp@localhost>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 23:12:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] PS4 (Wisp): WebAssembly SIMD and threads

JavaScriptCore keeps WebAssembly SIMD only on a CPU with AVX, and on the PS4 its check left SIMD off:
pages found none (Ruffle fell back to its plain module). The Jaguar has AVX and the whole program is
built for it (-march=btver2), so AVX is taken as present on the PS4; what CPUID said goes to the log
once.

Shared WebAssembly memories (WebAssembly threads) needed the fault signal handler, which the PS4 build
leaves off (the app's crash log owns SIGSEGV and SIGBUS): a module with one did not parse ("shared
memory is not enabled") and new WebAssembly.Memory({shared: true}) made a memory that was not shared.
With SharedArrayBuffer on (JSC_useSharedArrayBuffer, the app's setting) both work on the PS4,
bounds-checked. JavaScriptCore checks such a memory against its maximum, so an access between its size
and its maximum faults instead of trapping: only a module that reads past the end of its own memory
does that.

A growable bounds-checking memory (a shared memory, a resizable or growable ArrayBuffer) was its whole
maximum in memory from the start, the pages past its size protected: the PS4 puts memory behind every
mapping at once, and a game's shared memory has a maximum of 4 GiB. Now it is a reservation (address
space only: WTF's OSAllocator), whose pages get memory as the buffer grows (wtf_ps4_commit; no memory
left is an out-of-memory error, not a stop) and give it back as a resizable buffer shrinks.
---
